CORE
main.c
--unwind 17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ length of the copy: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^\[.*\.unwind\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^warning: ignoring
--
The need for --unwind here is undesirable: every loop of this program has a
constant bound, yet without --unwind symbolic execution does not end.  With
--malloc-may-fail (the default), the merge after dup's early return gives
each byte of the buffer the value `malloc failed ? unknown : copied byte',
because the merge at malloc's return made the buffer defined on both paths.
main's test `q == 0' excludes the failure path, but q's value
`malloc failed ? NULL : buffer' is not constant-propagated (an if with a
symbolic condition), so the condition is not carried into the reads of the
while loop, whose condition then never becomes false.  test_no_fail.desc
shows that the loop ends without --unwind when malloc cannot fail.  A fix
would propagate branch conditions into later reads (path-sensitive constant
propagation in goto-symex); then --unwind should be removed.  The same
pattern made the C++ front end's model of the MSVC runtime's
__std_exception_copy unwind without end (cpp11_msvc_exception_copy_of_copy).
